    tweetarticle: "Alberta Premier Danielle Smith has tabled a resolution in the Legislative Assembly invoking the Alberta Sovereignty Within a United Canada Act, ordering provincial grid operators to disregard federal 2035 Clean Electricity Regulations.\n\nReview Danielle Smith on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/danielle-smith\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Formally invokes the Alberta Sovereignty Act against federal Clean Electricity Regulations (CER).\n- Prohibits the Alberta Electric System Operator (AESO) and provincial utilities from enforcing federal gas-fired generation phase-out dates.\n- Authorizes the establishment of a provincial crown corporation to step in and operate thermal natural gas generating units if private utilities face federal sanctions.\n- Protects an estimated $12B in baseline natural gas electricity generation assets supplying 70% of Alberta's winter power.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Alberta Government & Energy Producers: Argue that Section 92A of the Constitution gives provinces exclusive jurisdiction over electricity and natural resources, warning that forced 2035 coal/gas bans cause deadly winter grid blackouts.\n- Federal Government & Environmental Organizations: Counter that federal environmental criminal law encompasses national greenhouse emission standards and pledge to defend federal regulatory authority in the Supreme Court of Canada.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Danielle Smith's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/danielle-smith\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/alberta-premier-danielle-smith-invokes-sovereignty-act-over-federal-clean-electricity-regulations-2026-08-25\n\n#Alberta #DanielleSmith #SovereigntyAct #CleanEnergy #Constitution #ABHealth #EnergyPoli #Choseno",
    breakingNews: true,
    body: "EDMONTON — Alberta Premier Danielle Smith introduced a sweeping resolution in the Legislative Assembly on Monday formally invoking the Alberta Sovereignty Within a United Canada Act against the federal government's Clean Electricity Regulations (CER), escalating a monumental constitutional standoff between Ottawa and Edmonton.\n\nThe resolution directs provincial entities—including the Alberta Electric System Operator (AESO) and the Alberta Utilities Commission (AUC)—to refuse compliance with federal mandates requiring Canada's electricity grid to achieve net-zero emissions by 2035.\n\nA Constitutional Battle Over Resource Jurisdiction\nAt the core of the dispute is the constitutional division of powers under Section 92A of the Constitution Act, 1982, which explicitly grants provinces exclusive legislative jurisdiction over the development, conservation, and management of non-renewable natural resources and electricity generation facilities.\n\nKey provisions of the Sovereignty Act resolution include:\n- Provincial Shield for Thermal Generation: Barring provincial regulators from shuttering modern combined-cycle natural gas power plants that entered commercial service after 2015.\n- Crown Corporation Power Generation: Authorizing the provincial cabinet to incorporate a provincial power generator of last resort to purchase and operate natural gas assets if private utilities are subjected to federal prosecution under the Canadian Environmental Protection Act (CEPA).\n- Legal Constitutional Challenge: Directing the Alberta Attorney General to refer the CER directly to the Alberta Court of Appeal to test its constitutional validity.\n\n\"Ottawa's ideological 2035 electricity target is unachievable, unconstitutional, and dangerous for Albertans who rely on reliable natural gas generation when temperatures drop below minus-thirty,\" Premier Danielle Smith said in the legislature. \"We will not allow federal overreach to bankrupt our industries, spike power bills for families, or leave our grid vulnerable to catastrophic blackouts. Alberta has the constitutional right to power its own future.\"\n\nFederal Environment Minister Steven Guilbeault strongly rejected the provincial move, stating that federal clean air and climate regulations are anchored in national peace, order, and good governance powers and the federal criminal law power to regulate toxic greenhouse emissions."
