    tweet: "The White House issues an executive directive ordering the FTC and DOJ to ban algorithmic rent-setting software across 12 major metro markets.",
    tweetarticle: "The White House has issued an executive directive instructing the FTC and the DOJ Antitrust Division to initiate formal rulemaking and enforcement actions banning algorithmic property management software that coordinates residential apartment rents across corporate landlords.\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Issues executive action targeting proprietary property management algorithms pooling non-public leasing data.\n- Classifies algorithmic competitor coordination as per se unlawful price-fixing under Section 1 of the Sherman Antitrust Act.\n- Impacts 16 million rental units nationwide where institutional landlords utilize algorithmic revenue management.\n- Aims to lower median apartment rent increases in major urban markets (Atlanta, Phoenix, Dallas, Seattle) by 4% to 7%.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Federal Regulators & Tenant Rights Coalitions: Emphasize that exchanging sensitive pricing data through automated algorithms creates corporate rent cartels, artificially keeping apartment vacancy rates high to maximize landlord profits.\n- Real Estate Software Vendors & Corporate Landlords: Argue that revenue management tools simply provide market supply-and-demand forecasting, helping landlords price units accurately and optimize building occupancy.\n\nCHOSENO — GOOGLE REVIEWS FOR DEMOCRACY & POLICY:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for democracy. Review public decisions, track government accountability, and share your rating on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/white-house-orders-ftc-and-doj-to-ban-algorithmic-tenant-rent-fixing-software-2026-08-25\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/white-house-orders-ftc-and-doj-to-ban-algorithmic-tenant-rent-fixing-software-2026-08-25\n\n#WhiteHouse #Antitrust #FTC #DOJ #HousingAffordability #RentPrices #ShermanAct #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "WASHINGTON — The White House on Monday issued a comprehensive executive directive ordering the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) and the Department of Justice (DOJ) Antitrust Division to intensify federal enforcement and draft statutory rules prohibiting commercial real estate algorithms from pooling non-public pricing data to coordinate apartment rental rates.\n\nThe action targets property management software platforms utilized by institutional landlords to manage millions of multifamily apartment units in high-growth metropolitan areas including Atlanta, Phoenix, Dallas, Denver, and Seattle.\n\nUnmasking the Algorithmic Rent Cartel\nFederal antitrust investigators found that algorithmic pricing platforms collect daily, non-public transactional data—including actual executed lease rates, renewal terms, and unit vacancy numbers—from competing landlords. The software algorithms then calculate automated daily pricing recommendations that participating landlords adopt at compliance rates exceeding 90%.\n\nThe White House directive establishes:\n- Per Se Unlawful Price-Fixing Standard: Clarifying DOJ enforcement guidelines that sharing non-public data through a centralized algorithmic intermediary constitutes unlawful horizontal collusion under Section 1 of the Sherman Act.\n- Injunction and Civil Penalty Actions: Directing federal prosecutors to seek structural remedies, including mandatory divestitures and financial restitution for affected tenants.\n- State Attorney General Coordination: Establishing a joint federal-state enforcement task force with sixteen state Attorneys General already investigating residential rent inflation.\n\n\"No American family should have their rent jacked up by secret algorithms working on behalf of corporate landlords who collude behind closed doors,\" a senior administration official said during a briefing. \"When technology is used to eliminate competition and fix prices, that is not innovation—it is illegal price-fixing, and the federal government is using every tool available to stop it.\"\n\nNational apartment association executives and real estate tech companies strongly contested the federal characterization, asserting that revenue management software operates as dynamic pricing algorithms analogous to airline and hotel booking systems, reflecting true market equilibrium rather than collusion."
