    body: "VICTORIA, B.C. — Premier David Eby and Minister of Public Safety Mike Farnworth announced on Monday that the Legislative Assembly of British Columbia has granted royal assent to the Restricting Public Consumption of Illegal Substances Amendment Act, establishing strict province-wide prohibitions on the open-air use of unregulated illicit drugs in municipal parks, beaches, sports fields, and transit hubs.\n\nThe legislation responds directly to sustained outrage from municipal mayors, parents, and business associations following the rollout of BC's three-year federal decriminalization pilot project, which decriminalized possession of small amounts of illicit opioids, cocaine, methamphetamine, and MDMA.\n\nRestoring Civic Boundaries in Public Spaces\nThe statutory framework provides:\n- 15-Meter Buffer Zones: Strictly banning illicit drug consumption within 15 meters of playgrounds, spray parks, wading pools, skate parks, and primary school entrances.\n- Police Enforcement and Seizure Powers: Authorizing municipal police forces and RCMP officers to order individuals consuming drugs in prohibited zones to cease consumption, relocate, or face immediate confiscation and lawful arrest for non-compliance.\n- Funding for Dedicated Supervised Consumption Sites: Appropriating $45 million to regional health authorities (Vancouver Coastal Health, Fraser Health, Island Health) to open supervised inhalation sites equipped with medical oxygen and naloxone.\n\n\"British Columbia is committed to treating addiction as a health issue rather than a criminal one, but compassion cannot come at the expense of community safety and public order,\" Premier David Eby said during a press conference at the legislature. \"Families, children, and seniors have a fundamental right to enjoy their neighborhood parks, playgrounds, and bus stops without witnessing open drug use or stepping over hazardous waste. This law sets clear, common-sense rules for our public spaces.\"\n\nHarm reduction non-profits and civil liberties organizations criticized the legislation, warning that spatial bans risk driving users into isolated back alleys where overdose response times are slower."
