    tweet: "The FEC issues landmark bipartisan rules requiring political campaigns and super PACs to clearly disclose AI deepfakes in ads.",
    tweetarticle: "The Federal Election Commission has finalized bipartisan regulations under the Federal Election Campaign Act, requiring all political campaigns, super PACs, and digital committees to prominently disclose AI-generated synthetic audio and video in campaign advertisements.\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Mandates 4-second unmissable visual disclaimers and spoken audio notices on any political ad featuring generative AI avatars or cloned voices.\n- Prohibits deceptive synthetic media designed to mislead voters regarding candidate statements or voting procedures.\n- Establishes expedited 48-hour FEC enforcement procedures for emergency preliminary injunctions during the final 60 days before general elections.\n- Imposes civil campaign finance fines up to $50,000 per violation.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Bipartisan FEC Commissioners & Election Watchdogs: Emphasize that undisclosed hyper-realistic deepfakes threaten the integrity of democratic elections by making it impossible for voters to distinguish authentic candidate statements from fabrications.\n- Digital Advertising Trade Groups: Support transparency while urging clear technical standards to avoid penalizing routine digital video color-grading and audio noise reduction.\n\nCHOSENO — GOOGLE REVIEWS FOR DEMOCRACY & POLICY:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for democracy. Review public decisions, track government accountability, and share your rating on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/fec-issues-landmark-transparency-rules-mandating-ai-deepfake-disclosures-in-campaign-ads-2026-08-25\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/fec-issues-landmark-transparency-rules-mandating-ai-deepfake-disclosures-in-campaign-ads-2026-08-25\n\n#FEC #Elections #ArtificialIntelligence #Deepfakes #CampaignFinance #Politics #Democracy #Choseno",
    breakingNews: true,
    body: "WASHINGTON — In a unanimous bipartisan vote, the Federal Election Commission (FEC) on Monday approved comprehensive new regulations governing the use of generative artificial intelligence, cloned audio voices, and synthetic video in federal political campaign advertising.\n\nThe landmark regulatory action resolves months of legal debate over the application of the Federal Election Campaign Act's fraudulent misrepresentation statutes (52 U.S.C. § 30124) to rapidly proliferating digital AI tools ahead of the midterm and general election cycles.\n\nMandating Transparency for Synthetic Political Ads\nThe new administrative rules establish:\n- Prominent Visual & Audio Disclaimers: Television, streaming, and digital video ads containing AI-altered candidate depictions must display a clear, readable text banner (\"Contains AI-generated synthetic audio or video\") for at least four seconds, accompanied by a spoken audio disclaimer.\n- Prohibition on Deceptive Synthetic Speech: Explicitly classifying the unauthorized cloning of an opposing candidate's voice to generate fabricated policy stances or fraudulent withdrawal announcements as unlawful misrepresentation.\n- Rapid Administrative Injunction Process: Enabling the FEC to issue emergency expedited advisory opinions and refer deceptive synthetic media cases directly to federal district courts within 48 hours during peak campaign windows.\n\n\"Voters have a fundamental right to know whether the words they hear coming out of a candidate's mouth were actually spoken by that candidate or fabricated by an algorithm in a digital studio,\" the FEC Chairman said in a statement following the vote. \"These rules protect freedom of speech while ensuring that democratic elections remain rooted in truth and transparency.\"\n\nFirst Amendment scholars and digital democracy coalitions commended the commission, noting that establishing clear disclaimer requirements protects political satire while preventing catastrophic election eve disinformation."
