    seoTitle: "Marc Miller Tightens Canada Student Visas & Work Permits | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "Immigration Minister Marc Miller reduces international student study permits by 10% to relieve municipal housing pressure.",
    tweet: "Immigration Minister Marc Miller announces a further 10% reduction in international student study permits and restricts work visas in Canada.",
    tweetarticle: "Immigration Minister Marc Miller and Prime Minister Justin Trudeau have announced tightened federal immigration rules, reducing international study permits by an additional 10% and restricting post-graduate work permits.\n\nReview Marc Miller on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/marc-miller\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Reduces national study permit target to 437,000 for 2026, representing a cumulative 35% reduction from 2023 levels.\n- Restricts Post-Graduation Work Permit (PGWP) eligibility to graduates from public college and university programs linked directly to national labor shortages.\n- Eliminates spousal open work permits for partners of students in non-doctoral programs.\n- Aims to relieve intense rental vacancy strain and emergency healthcare wait times in Toronto, Vancouver, Montreal, and Waterloo.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Federal Government & Urban Mayors: Emphasize that temporary resident numbers grew at an unsustainable pace, overwhelming rental housing markets and undermining the integrity of Canada's post-secondary education brand.\n- University Presidents & Business Chambers: Warn that sharp visa cuts create severe tuition budget deficits for public universities and exacerbate labor shortages in retail and hospitality sectors.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Marc Miller's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/marc-miller\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/immigration-minister-marc-miller-tightens-international-student-caps-and-work-permits-2026-08-25\n\n#Canada #MarcMiller #JustinTrudeau #Immigration #HousingCrisis #PostSecondary #CdnPoli #Economy #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "OTTAWA — Minister of Immigration, Refugees and Citizenship Marc Miller announced on Monday that the federal government will implement a further 10% reduction in new international student study permits for 2026, capping the intake target at 437,000 permits and imposing strict language proficiency and labor-market alignment rules on Post-Graduation Work Permits (PGWP).\n\nThe announcement represents the latest major structural recalibration in Canada's immigration architecture, aiming to curb unsustainable temporary resident growth that has placed extraordinary strain on municipal rental housing vacancy, urban transit corridors, and primary healthcare clinics in university cities across the country.\n\nRestoring Balance to the International Student Program\nUnder the revised statutory framework implemented through Immigration, Refugees and Citizenship Canada (IRCC):\n- Reduced Study Permit Target: Setting the 2026 cap at 437,000 study permits, including master's and doctoral degree applicants previously exempt from provincial attestation quotas.\n- Labor-Market PGWP Alignment: Restricting post-graduate work permit eligibility exclusively to graduates in fields with verified national labor shortages—such as healthcare, STEM disciplines, skilled building trades, and agriculture.\n- Spousal Work Permit Restrictions: Limiting spousal open work permits exclusively to spouses of foreign students enrolled in master's programs of at least 16 months duration or doctoral degree programs.\n\n\"Immigration is essential to Canada's economic future, but our temporary resident programs grew at an unsustainable rate that opened the door to bad actors exploiting students and strained our local housing and healthcare systems,\" Minister Marc Miller said during a press conference in Ottawa. \"By right-sizing these programs and tightening work permit rules, we are protecting the integrity of our world-class educational institutions and ensuring that newcomers can access the housing and services they need to succeed.\"\n\nColleges and Institutes Canada and municipal chambers of commerce expressed concern over revenue losses, urging the federal government to work with provinces to increase core public funding for higher education."
