    metaDescription: "Texas PUC orders massive AI data centers to secure on-site power or face mandatory curtailment during peak demand.",
    tweet: "Texas PUC and ERCOT enact emergency rules requiring large AI data centers to install on-site power or face curtailment during heatwaves.",
    tweetarticle: "The Public Utility Commission of Texas and ERCOT have enacted binding reliability standards requiring AI data centers drawing over 75 megawatts to install on-site dispatchable generation or submit to automated emergency grid curtailment.\n\nReview Greg Abbott on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/greg-abbott-governor\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Approves ERCOT Nodal Protocol Revision Request mandating dynamic load-reduction capabilities for hyper-scale data centers.\n- Prevents 40 gigawatts of planned AI compute facilities from overloading Texas residential electric grids.\n- Protects Texas residential ratepayers from bearing the multi-billion dollar cost of transmission line upgrades exclusively serving tech hubs.\n- Mandates that data center campuses maintain 48 hours of on-site continuous backup generation (gas turbines or battery storage).\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Texas Regulators & Consumer Advocates: Highlight that unchecked crypto and AI compute demand risks triggering widespread blackout emergencies unless data centers participate directly in grid resilience.\n- Tech Data Center Developers: Warn that strict co-generation mandates increase construction lead times by 18 months and threaten Texas's leadership in the national AI compute boom.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Greg Abbott's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/greg-abbott-governor\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/texas-puc-and-ercot-impose-strict-reliability-rules-on-ai-data-centers-2026-08-25\n\n#Texas #GregAbbott #ERCOT #EnergyGrid #ArtificialIntelligence #DataCenters #Economy #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "AUSTIN, Texas — The Public Utility Commission of Texas (PUC) and the Electric Reliability Council of Texas (ERCOT) voted unanimously on Monday to enact comprehensive emergency reliability rules governing large flexible loads, requiring hyper-scale artificial intelligence data centers and crypto-mining facilities connecting to the Texas electrical grid to install dedicated on-site power generation or face mandatory automated curtailment during peak summer heatwaves.\n\nThe regulatory intervention addresses explosive growth in industrial electricity demand across Texas, where ERCOT forecasts indicate that data center power requests could exceed 40,000 megawatts by 2030—nearly half of the state's current peak summer demand.\n\nProtecting the Texas Power Grid from AI Compute Surges\nUnder the newly finalized commission order:\n- Mandatory Curtailment Protocols: Data center facilities demanding over 75 megawatts must install automated telemetric breaker switches allowing ERCOT grid controllers to ramp down facility electricity consumption within fifteen minutes during grid emergency alerts.\n- On-Site Backup Requirements: New commercial data center campuses must co-locate dispatchable on-site power generation—such as natural gas aeroderivative turbines or utility-scale battery energy storage systems—capable of sustaining operations for up to 48 hours.\n- Interconnection Cost Allocation: Prohibiting transmission utilities from shifting high-voltage substation interconnection construction expenses onto general residential ratepayer utility bills.\n\n\"Texas welcomes technological innovation and business investment, but our primary constitutional responsibility is to keep the lights on for Texas families, hospitals, and small businesses,\" PUC Chairman Thomas Gleeson said following the vote. \"These rules ensure that massive AI data centers pay their fair share for grid infrastructure and act as shock absorbers during extreme weather, rather than overwhelming our grid.\"\n\nTech industry associations and global cloud providers argued that rigid co-location requirements could cause developers to shift planned billion-dollar data center investments to neighboring states."
