    seoTitle: "TTC & ATU Reach Tentative 3-Year Labor Contract | Choseno",
    metaDescription: "TTC and ATU Local 113 reach tentative 3-year labor agreement, averting a citywide transit shutdown for 1.3M daily riders.",
    tweet: "The TTC and ATU Local 113 reach a tentative 3-year labor agreement, averting a citywide transit shutdown in Toronto.",
    tweetarticle: "The Toronto Transit Commission (TTC) and ATU Local 113 have reached a tentative three-year collective agreement, averting a strike that would have halted subway, streetcar, and bus service for 1.3 million daily riders across Toronto.\n\nReview Olivia Chow on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/olivia-chow\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Averts the first full TTC transit strike in 16 years following round-the-clock weekend bargaining.\n- Secures a 13.5% compounded wage increase over three years and enhanced frontline operator safety protocols.\n- Protects job security against third-party subcontracting across Line 5 Eglinton and Line 6 Finch West LRT maintenance.\n- City Council and union members prepare for formal ratification votes within two weeks.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Mayor Olivia Chow & Transit Workers: Highlight that fair wages and job security are vital to retaining experienced operators and mechanics, ensuring safe and reliable transit for working families.\n- Municipal Budget Analysts: Commend the averted shutdown while noting that higher operating labor expenses will require sustained provincial transit operating subsidies to avoid future fare hikes.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Olivia Chow's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/olivia-chow\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/toronto-transit-commission-and-atu-local-113-reach-tentative-three-year-labor-contract-2026-08-25\n\n#Toronto #OliviaChow #TTC #ATU113 #PublicTransit #Labor #Subway #ONPoli #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "TORONTO — The Toronto Transit Commission (TTC) and the Amalgamated Transit Union (ATU) Local 113 announced on Monday that they have reached a tentative three-year collective bargaining agreement, successfully averting a midnight strike deadline that would have completely paralyzed the transit network serving over 1.3 million daily commuters in Canada's largest city.\n\nThe settlement follows months of intense negotiations and marks the first collective agreement negotiated since Ontario courts struck down provincial legislation that unconstitutionally designated the TTC as an essential service, restoring workers' legal right to strike.\n\nAverting Citywide Gridlock\nA full shutdown of the TTC subway, streetcar, and bus fleet would have caused unprecedented traffic gridlock across the Greater Toronto Area and cost the regional economy an estimated $50 million per day in lost commercial productivity.\n\nKey terms of the tentative agreement include:\n- 13.5% Compounded Wage Increase: Providing phased annual wage adjustments recognizing rising living costs across the GTA.\n- Subcontracting and Job Protections: Securing binding protections that ensure maintenance and technical operations on new transit lines—including Line 5 Eglinton Crosstown and Line 6 Finch West—remain staffed by in-house union technicians.\n- Operator Safety & De-Escalation Shield: Expanding protective safety enclosures on all surface buses and streetcars, paired with dedicated crisis de-escalation response teams across high-density subway stations.\n\n\"Toronto riders and workers can breathe a huge sigh of relief today,\" Mayor Olivia Chow said during a morning press conference at City Hall. \"Our transit workers do heroic work every single day keeping our city moving. By listening to each other and negotiating in good faith, we reached a fair deal that respects transit workers, keeps fares affordable, and ensures reliable service for millions of Torontonians.\"\n\nUnion leadership unanimously endorsed the settlement, with ratification voting scheduled for all 11,500 active members over the coming days."
