    tweetarticle: "US District Judge Amit Mehta has scheduled a landmark remedies hearing in Washington following his historic ruling that Big Tech search practices constitute an illegal monopoly under Section 2 of the Sherman Act.\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Enters the high-stakes remedies phase following the landmark August liability ruling.\n- Evaluates DOJ proposals to prohibit $26B in annual revenue-sharing default agreements with Apple, Samsung, and browser developers.\n- Considers structural remedies, including mandatory divestiture of the Android operating system or Chrome browser.\n- Marks the most consequential federal antitrust corporate restructuring trial since the Microsoft case in 2000.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Department of Justice & Antitrust Enforcers: Maintain that default contract bans and data-sharing remedies are essential to break entrenched digital barriers and allow competing search engines and AI assistants to innovate.\n- Corporate Defense Counsel & Tech Economists: Argue that consumers choose dominant search engines because of product quality, warning that forced structural breakups will degrade user convenience and security.\n\nCHOSENO — GOOGLE REVIEWS FOR DEMOCRACY & POLICY:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for democracy. Review public decisions, track government accountability, and share your rating on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/federal-judge-orders-remedies-hearing-in-landmark-search-engine-monopoly-ruling-2026-08-25\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/federal-judge-orders-remedies-hearing-in-landmark-search-engine-monopoly-ruling-2026-08-25\n\n#Antitrust #DOJ #BigTech #ShermanAct #Monopoly #DigitalEconomy #FederalCourt #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "WASHINGTON — United States District Judge Amit P. Mehta on Monday issued a comprehensive procedural scheduling order outlining the calendar for the high-stakes remedies phase in the landmark antitrust case United States v. Google, following the court's historic determination that the tech giant maintained an illegal monopoly in general search services and general text advertising.\n\nThe forthcoming remedies trial, scheduled to commence in early 2027, represents the most consequential judicial restructuring of a dominant American technology corporation in more than a generation, carrying profound implications for the global digital economy, mobile operating systems, and artificial intelligence search architectures.\n\nStructural Divestitures vs. Behavioral Prohibitions\nIn court filings submitted to the US District Court for the District of Columbia, the Department of Justice and a coalition of thirty-eight state Attorneys General outlined a menu of potential equitable remedies:\n- Prohibiting Default Distribution Agreements: Barring the company from paying billions annually to smartphone manufacturers, telecom carriers, and browser developers (such as Apple and Mozilla) to secure exclusive pre-installed default search engine status.\n- Choice Screens and Interoperability: Mandating the rollout of prominent choice screens on mobile devices allowing users to select default search engines and AI assistants upon device activation.\n- Potential Structural Divestiture: Reviewing whether behavioral restrictions are sufficient or whether structural separation—such as spinning off the Chrome web browser or Android open-source ecosystem—is necessary to restore competitive market dynamics.\n\n\"The court has found liability under Section 2 of the Sherman Act, and the task now is to fashion an effective, durable remedy that terminates the illegal monopoly, prevents its recurrence, and restores competition to the digital marketplace,\" federal prosecutors wrote in their submission.\n\nDefense attorneys argued that consumers utilize dominant search platforms because of superior speed, indexing scale, and user experience, cautioning that punitive structural remedies would harm consumers and stifle American leadership in artificial intelligence development."
