    tweet: "The US 5th Circuit Court of Appeals upholds federal authority over the Rio Grande, ordering Texas to reposition floating border buoys.",
    tweetarticle: "The US 5th Circuit Court of Appeals has issued an en banc ruling upholding a federal injunction against Texas Governor Greg Abbott, ruling that the federal government retains exclusive jurisdiction over navigable international waterways under the Rivers and Harbors Act.\n\nReview Greg Abbott on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/greg-abbott-governor\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Orders Texas to reposition 1,000 feet of rotating marine barrier buoys onto the Texas riverbank in Eagle Pass.\n- Reaffirms Section 10 of the Rivers and Harbors Appropriation Act of 1899 requiring federal Army Corps of Engineers permits for waterway obstructions.\n- Rejects Texas's constitutional claim that unauthorized border crossings constitute an \"invasion\" under Article I, Section 10.\n- Texas Attorney General Ken Paxton vows immediate appeal to the US Supreme Court.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Department of Justice & Diplomatic Envoys: Argue that unilateral state river barriers disrupt international navigation, violate 1944 bilateral treaties with Mexico, and risk diplomatic relations.\n- Texas Leadership & Border Sheriffs: Contend that the federal administration's failure to enforce border laws forces border states to exercise self-defense sovereign rights under the Texas Constitution.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Greg Abbott's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/greg-abbott-governor\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/federal-appeals-court-upholds-federal-authority-over-texas-rio-grande-floating-barriers-2026-08-25\n\n#Texas #GregAbbott #FifthCircuit #BorderSecurity #RioGrande #Immigration #Constitution #Choseno",
    breakingNews: true,
    body: "NEW ORLEANS — The United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit on Monday issued an en banc decision upholding a federal district court injunction that ordered Texas Governor Greg Abbott and the Texas Department of Public Safety (DPS) to reposition a 1,000-foot floating marine barrier installed in the middle of the Rio Grande near Eagle Pass, Texas.\n\nThe 10–7 ruling marks a major victory for the US Department of Justice and the State Department, affirming that the federal government holds paramount constitutional and statutory authority over international waterways and foreign relations.\n\nNavigable Waters and Constitutional Preemption\nThe majority opinion, authored by Chief Judge Priscilla Richman, held that the State of Texas violated Section 10 of the Rivers and Harbors Appropriation Act of 1899 by deploying heavy concrete anchors, steel cables, and circular saw blades in a navigable river without prior authorization from the US Army Corps of Engineers.\n\nKey elements of the ruling establish:\n- Federal Treaty Supremacy: Unilateral state installations violate the 1944 Water Treaty and the 1970 Boundary Treaty between the United States and Mexico, which require binational International Boundary and Water Commission (IBWC) approval.\n- Rejection of State 'Invasion' Power: The court rejected Texas's constitutional theory that irregular migrant crossings trigger the State Self-Defense Clause (Article I, Section 10, Clause 3), ruling that the clause applies exclusively to hostile armed foreign state forces.\n- Order to Relocate: Texas must move the floating buoys to the dry embankment along the Texas shore within ten business days.\n\n\"Texas has every right to defend its citizens and enforce state laws, but it cannot override the United States Constitution or seize exclusive federal control over an international boundary river shared with a foreign nation,\" the majority wrote.\n\nGovernor Greg Abbott and Texas Attorney General Ken Paxton sharply criticized the ruling, announcing an emergency petition for writ of certiorari to the United States Supreme Court.\n\n\"Texas will not back down in our fight to secure our southern border against transnational cartels and unlawful crossings while the federal administration refuses to do its job,\" Governor Abbott said in a statement. \"We will take this fight directly to the Supreme Court.\""
