    tweet: "Governors Gavin Newsom and Jay Inslee sign a bilateral accord linking California and Washington cap-and-invest carbon markets.",
    tweetarticle: "California Governor Gavin Newsom and Washington Governor Jay Inslee have signed a landmark bilateral interstate agreement linking their state carbon cap-and-invest markets and establishing unified Western electrical grid transmission tariffs.\n\nReview Gavin Newsom on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/gavin-newsom\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Formally links the California Cap-and-Trade program with Washington's Climate Commitment Act market.\n- Creates the largest carbon compliance market in North America, covering 48 million residents.\n- Lowers carbon allowance compliance costs for industrial manufacturers and utilities by expanding market liquidity.\n- Dedicates combined auction proceeds of $4.5B annually toward regional grid transmission and forest wildfire management.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Western Governors & Clean Energy Economists: Highlight that a unified carbon market lowers consumer energy volatility, accelerates regional solar/hydro sharing, and sets a national model for state-led climate action.\n- Mountain State Critics & Fossil Fuel Coalitions: Warn that linked carbon pricing risks raising diesel and gasoline transport fuel prices and explore potential interstate Commerce Clause challenges.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Gavin Newsom's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/gavin-newsom\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/california-and-washington-governors-sign-western-carbon-market-and-grid-pact-2026-08-25\n\n#California #Washington #GavinNewsom #JayInslee #CarbonMarket #CleanEnergy #Economy #Politics #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "SACRAMENTO — California Governor Gavin Newsom and Washington Governor Jay Inslee signed a landmark bilateral agreement on Monday formally linking the California Cap-and-Trade carbon allowance auction system with Washington's Climate Commitment Act market, creating the largest shared compliance carbon market in North America and standardizing cross-border renewable electricity transmission protocols across the West Coast.\n\nThe pact represents the culmination of two years of technical negotiations between the California Air Resources Board (CARB) and the Washington Department of Ecology, harmonizing statutory emissions reporting, allowance holding limits, and joint quarterly auctions set to launch in January 2027.\n\nScaling Western Clean Energy Markets\nCombining the two state programs establishes a compliance market representing an economy of over $4.5 trillion.\n\nKey pillars of the interstate accord include:\n- Joint Carbon Allowance Auctions: Creating unified quarterly auctions allowing regulated utilities, oil refineries, and industrial emitters in both states to purchase and trade verified carbon compliance instruments interchangeably.\n- Market Price Volatility Dampening: Expanding the total pool of market participants to prevent price spikes and reduce regulatory compliance overhead for regional manufacturers.\n- Interprovincial Expansion Pathway: Establishing statutory reciprocity enabling the Canadian province of Quebec, which is already linked with California through the Western Climate Initiative (WCI), to integrate with the Washington system.\n\n\"The West Coast is demonstrating that climate leadership and economic prosperity go hand in hand,\" Governor Gavin Newsom said during a joint announcement in San Francisco. \"By linking California and Washington's carbon markets, we are creating a broader, more resilient economic platform that accelerates clean energy investment, lowers compliance costs, and drives real accountability for corporate polluters.\"\n\nGovernor Jay Inslee added: \"When states unite to harness the power of market-based climate policy, we create a powerful gravitational pull that drives clean energy innovation across our entire region.\"\n\nOpponents in neighboring resource-dependent western states expressed concern over potential fuel cost impacts on cross-border trucking, indicating that legal coalitions are reviewing whether the pact requires formal congressional consent under Article I, Section 10 of the US Constitution."
