    tweet: "Premier Doug Ford tables legislation prohibiting Ontario municipalities from imposing local land transfer taxes on homebuyers.",
    tweetarticle: "Ontario Premier Doug Ford and Municipal Affairs Minister Paul Calandra have introduced the Protecting Homebuyers Act to legally prohibit all Ontario municipalities from creating municipal land transfer taxes.\n\nReview Doug Ford on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/doug-ford\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Introduces provincial legislation stripping municipalities of the statutory authority to levy local land transfer taxes.\n- Protects first-time homebuyers from paying double land transfer taxes when buying properties in Mississauga, Brampton, Ottawa, or Hamilton.\n- Saves the average homebuyer an estimated $12,000 to $18,000 in closing cost fees.\n- Grandfathers the existing City of Toronto Municipal Land Transfer Tax (MLTT) while prohibiting any rate expansions.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Provincial Government & Real Estate Boards: Highlight that runaway municipal closing taxes create an insurmountable financial barrier for young families trying to achieve homeownership.\n- Big-City Mayors & Association of Municipalities of Ontario (AMO): Argue that the province is restricting municipal revenue independence without providing alternative provincial funding to maintain local transit and roads.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review Doug Ford's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/doug-ford\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/ontario-premier-doug-ford-tables-legislation-banning-municipal-land-transfer-taxes-2026-08-25\n\n#Ontario #DougFord #PaulCalandra #HousingAffordability #ONPoli #RealEstate #Taxes #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "QUEEN'S PARK, Toronto — Premier Doug Ford and Minister of Municipal Affairs and Housing Paul Calandra introduced the Protecting Homebuyers and Building More Homes Act at Queen's Park on Monday, statutory legislation that legally preempts and bars all Ontario municipal councils from implementing municipal land transfer taxes on residential real estate transactions.\n\nThe legislative move shuts down active policy proposals in several major Ontario cities—including Mississauga, Brampton, Hamilton, and Ottawa—where municipal staff had recommended exploring municipal land transfer taxes to plug multi-million dollar structural budget shortfalls.\n\nEliminating Closing-Cost Friction for Homebuyers\nUnder existing provincial statutes, the City of Toronto was granted unique powers under the City of Toronto Act, 2006, to levy its own Municipal Land Transfer Tax (MLTT), effectively doubling closing tax costs compared to the rest of the province.\n\nThe Ford government's legislation:\n- Preempts Municipal Tax Powers: Explicitly amends the Municipal Act, 2001, to prohibit any local government outside Toronto from introducing a real estate transfer tax or transaction surcharge.\n- Capping Toronto's Tax Expansion: Restricts the City of Toronto from increasing current MLTT tax brackets on entry-level and mid-tier residential properties.\n- Mandating Accelerated Subdivision Approvals: Requires municipal planning departments to issue standard residential building permits within 45 days for standardized floorplans.\n\n\"The dream of homeownership is slipping out of reach for too many young families, and the last thing first-time buyers need is municipal councils adding tens of thousands of dollars in new taxes at the closing table,\" Premier Doug Ford said in the legislature. \"Our government is standing up for homebuyers. We are putting a stop to municipal land transfer taxes across Ontario, keeping costs down, and ensuring families can keep more of their hard-earned money.\"\n\nThe Association of Municipalities of Ontario (AMO) and regional mayors expressed strong frustration, warning that capping municipal revenue tools will force cities to either raise residential property taxes significantly or defer vital water, sewer, and road repairs."
