    tweetarticle: "Quebec Premier François Legault and the Office québécois de la langue française (OQLF) have initiated strict enforcement of Bill 96 regulations, requiring all commercial storefronts and trademark signs to feature at least two-thirds French text.\n\nReview François Legault on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/francois-legault\n\nWHAT CHANGED & TAXPAYER IMPACT:\n- Enforces strict commercial signage amendments to the Charter of the French Language (Bill 96).\n- Requires all non-French registered trademarks on outdoor facades to be accompanied by prominent French descriptions occupying at least twice the visual space.\n- Authorizes OQLF inspectors to issue escalating daily fines ranging from $3,000 to $30,000 per violation.\n- Affects over 12,000 international and national retail storefronts across Montreal, Quebec City, and Gatineau.\n\nTHE DEBATE:\n- Coalition Avenir Québec (CAQ) Government: Maintains that protecting the French language in North America requires visual predominance in public commercial spaces, halting the Anglicization of Montreal.\n- Retail Council of Canada & International Brands: Argue that forcing companies to redesign trademarked global facades creates costly logistical burdens and risks deterring foreign retail investment in Quebec.\n\nNOW YOU HAVE THE SAY — CHOSENO:\nChoseno is like Google Reviews for politicians. Don't just watch decisions happen from the sidelines — now you have the say. Review François Legault's record, speak your mind, and let your fellow constituents know where you stand on his official public wall:\nhttps://choseno.com/wall/francois-legault\n\nRead the full investigative report on Choseno:\nhttps://choseno.com/news/quebec-premier-francois-legault-enforces-strict-french-commercial-signage-mandates-2026-08-25\n\n#Quebec #FrançoisLegault #Bill96 #LanguageLaws #OQLF #QCPoli #Montreal #Economy #Choseno",
    breakingNews: false,
    body: "QUEBEC CITY — Premier François Legault and Minister of the French Language Jean-François Roberge announced on Monday the formal commencement of rigorous compliance enforcement under Bill 96 amendments to the Charter of the French Language, mandating that all commercial storefronts, trademark logos, and advertising displays across Quebec ensure that French text is \"markedly predominant\"—occupying at least two-thirds of total sign area.\n\nThe enforcement deadline follows a two-year regulatory grace period, targeting major international and national retail chains operating in downtown Montreal, suburban shopping centers, and commercial business parks.\n\nThe Mechanics of Commercial Language Enforcement\nUnder the modernized regulations enforced by the Office québécois de la langue française (OQLF):\n- Trademark Descriptions: While recognized non-French brand trademarks (e.g. \"Best Buy\", \"Apple\", \"Costco\") remain protected, they must now be flanked by permanent, prominent French generic descriptors (e.g. \"Magasin d'électronique\", \"Produits informatiques\") that are twice as visually prominent.\n- On-Site Inspection Taskforce: OQLF has deployed fifty specialized language inspectors to conduct storefront compliance audits in high-density commercial corridors.\n- Strict Financial Penalties: Non-compliant businesses face progressive daily administrative fines ranging from $3,000 for a first corporate offense up to $30,000 per day for repeated infractions.\n\n\"French is the only official language of Quebec, and it must be the common, welcoming language that everyone sees when walking down our commercial avenues,\" Premier François Legault said during an address in Quebec City. \"Quebec is the only French-speaking nation in North America. Defending our language is not just a policy—it is a duty to our history, our culture, and our children. We expect all businesses operating in Quebec to respect our laws.\"\n\nThe Retail Council of Canada and international chambers of commerce raised practical concerns regarding manufacturing supply chain delays for custom illuminated signage, requesting reasonable administrative flexibility for small franchise owners."
